  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nConvective chances linger across mainly our north tonight as a \ncold front sinks southward. IR tops to -75C are impressive and \nwe've seen some gustiness out of recent storms just upstream. \nWe'll have to keep an eye out for any flare-ups but overall the \ntrending toward daybreak is the other direction wrt svr, and \npops should be waning by 12Z if not completely departed; however\nthere will continue to be a localized heavy rain/flooding \nhazard where training occurs and isolated gusty winds cannot be \ncompletely ruled out. All of this amidst fropa and winds \nshifting to the northwest will provide much needed relief from \nthe extreme heat, as temps return to more climatologically \nnormal readings with highs in the upper half 80s to near 90 \ndegrees. Dew points draw down into the lower 70s north but may \nhang and hover in the mid 70s in our south until toward the \nclose of the day. Even so, with the lower temps, the heat index \nacross our south stays just below Advisory criteria.\n\nSurface high pressure builds overtop the PAH FA Tuesday and\nreally draws the lower dew points down on in and across the\narea; seeing some mid 60s isodrosotherms (gasp?!) may not be \nentirely out of the question during the Tuesday afternoon \nmix-out under near cloudless skies. \n\nAs the high shifts east Tuesday night, we'll start to see some\nreturn warmth/moisture heading into the mid week as upper air \nenergy buckles the pattern and evolves a wave that tracks/makes\nits Ohio Valley passage by week's end. The preceding time \ngetting there offers the best chance of a strong or severe \nstorm, namely Wednesday night into Thursday, but it appears to \nbe too low for any type of categorical mention at this writing; \nsomething to monitor/be cognizant toward. After this 2nd system's\nultimate passage, we see a nice and pleasant, reinforcing shot \nof cooler/drier air with a similar/repeat dew point plunge into \nthe 60s that finishes out our week.",
